usage: dots-edit [<entry> | <target> | <source file>]
       dots-edit --view <target> | <entry> [--as <os>[,<role>] | --matrix]
       dots-edit --list | --help

Edit any part of the dotfiles in the chezmoi source, then see what it changes.

  dots-edit                      pick a registry entry (.chezmoidata/edit.toml)
  dots-edit mcp                  edit an entry; dots-edit --list shows them all
  dots-edit ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md  edit what a target is made of: pick a node of its
                                 composition tree (layers, partials, data files)
  dots-edit dot_config/git/config.tmpl
                                 edit a source file by its source-relative path

Every edit renders the target state of this machine and of 9 configurations
(darwin, linux, windows x personal, work, light; every harness selected), opens
$EDITOR (inside tmux a side pane shows this machine's changes on every save), runs
the entry's check, renders again and lists the changed targets per
configuration. A configuration that stops rendering fails the edit: nothing is
applied. Then, for this machine only: chezmoi diff of the changed targets,
"apply now? [y/N]", and a reminder to ship a PR. Encrypted files are edited in
a private temp copy and re-encrypted only when changed.

  --view <target>                its composition tree and its rendered content
  --view <entry>                 its composition tree and the targets it reaches
  --as <os>[,<role>]             render one configuration instead of this machine:
                                 os darwin|linux|windows, role personal (default),
                                 work or light; a preview only, never applied
  --matrix                       every configuration: identical renders grouped,
                                 the others as diffs against the first
  --list                         the registry entries

Shortcuts: agents-edit, aliases-edit, ssh-edit (also ~/.ssh/config.d files).
Docs: docs/OS.md#editing-your-dotfiles
